], "alarm_features": ["Unintentional weight loss", "Hypercalcemia (constitutional/metabolic alarm sign)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Malignancy with Hypercalcemia", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.7359618879800699, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Weight loss and neurological symptoms provide weak evidence for malignancy-related hypercalcemia, though non-smoker status slightly reduces suspicion for lung …"], "evidence_against": ["Absence of RAG features (bone pain, white matter lesions, cardiac septal thickening) reduces malignancy suspicion, though hypercalcemia and weight loss remain …"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": ["humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "multiple myeloma", "squamous cell carcinoma", "primary hyperparathyroidism"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Medication-induced Hypercalcemia", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.001, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Non-smoker status and absence of milk-alkali syndrome features provide moderate evidence against medication-induced hypercalcemia"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Endocrine/Paraneoplastic Hypercalcemia Syndrome", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.193597250383354, "posterior": 0.22668568451599563, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Subacute neurological symptoms are consistent with paraneoplastic hypercalcemia, but absence of vitamin D excess (which would favor sarcoidosis/milk-alkali syn…"], "evidence_against": ["Absence of syndactyly and other paraneoplastic features slightly weakens the endocrine/paraneoplastic mechanism but does not exclude it given the subacute neur…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["primary hyperparathyroidism", "humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "milk-alkali syndrome", "sarcoidosis"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Primary Hyperparathyroidism", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.029, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Classic PHPT features are missing, providing moderate evidence against this diagnosis despite hypercalcemia and GERD."]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Reactive Hypercalcemia with Inflammatory Process", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.007, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Hematologic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Lymphoma, Multiple Myeloma)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.29438475519202795, "posterior": 0.29438475519202795, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": ["humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "multiple myeloma", "squamous cell carcinoma", "primary hyperparathyroidism"]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Solid Tumor with Paraneoplastic Hypercalcemia (e.g., Breast, Renal, Squamous Cell Carcinoma)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.22078856639402097, "posterior": 0.22078856639402097, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": ["primary hyperparathyroidism", "humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "milk-alkali syndrome", "sarcoidosis"]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Metastatic Bone Disease with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Breast, Prostate Cancer)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.22078856639402097, "posterior": 0.22078856639402097, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": ["primary hyperparathyroidism", "humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "milk-alkali syndrome", "sarcoidosis"]}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of unintentional weight loss, subacute neurological symptoms, and non-smoker status provide sufficient evidence for malignancy-related hypercalcemia?", "summary": "Weight loss and neurological symptoms provide weak evidence for malignancy-related hypercalcemia, though non-smoker status slightly reduces suspicion for lung cancer as a source."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute neurological presentation and absence of vitamin D excess support Endocrine/Paraneoplastic Hypercalcemia Syndrome over other hypercalcemia causes?", "summary": "Subacute neurological symptoms are consistent with paraneoplastic hypercalcemia, but absence of vitamin D excess (which would favor sarcoidosis/milk-alkali syndrome) slightly weakens this mechanism."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the history of poorly controlled GERD and low 24-hour urinary sodium (5 mmol/24h) support Primary Hyperparathyroidism as a potential cause of hypercalcemia?", "summary": "Low urinary sodium is inconsistent with primary hyperparathyroidism (which typically causes hypercalciuria), making it less likely despite GERD and hypercalcemia."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of bone pain, abnormal white matter findings, and cardiac septal abnormalities (RAG features favoring malignancy) disfavor Malignancy with Hypercalcemia despite hypercalcemia and wei…", "summary": "Absence of RAG features (bone pain, white matter lesions, cardiac septal thickening) reduces malignancy suspicion, though hypercalcemia and weight loss remain concerning."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the lack of 2-3 toe syndactyly or other endocrine/paraneoplastic features sufficient to disfavor Endocrine/Paraneoplastic Hypercalcemia Syndrome despite subacute neurological symptoms?", "summary": "Absence of syndactyly and other paraneoplastic features slightly weakens the endocrine/paraneoplastic mechanism but does not exclude it given the subacute neurological presentation."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the absence of renal calculi, osteoporosis, and polyuria/diabetes insipidus (classic PHPT features) disfavor Primary Hyperparathyroidism despite hypercalcemia and GERD?", "summary": "Classic PHPT features are missing, providing moderate evidence against this diagnosis despite hypercalcemia and GERD."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.671], ["B3", 0.194], ["B4", 0.095]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.736], ["B1.1", 0.294], ["B3", 0.227]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "Patient remains hemodynamically stable (BP 115/71, HR 72, RR 12), with no airway compromise, acute respiratory distress, or neurological deterioration requiring immediate intervention. Hypercalcemia (12.5 mg/dL) is present but does not show acute end-organ damage (e.g., renal failure, cardiac instability) necessitating emergent treatment.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch (B1.1) has posterior < 0.75 and dangerous alternative B3 (danger=0.7, posterior=0.194) remains unresolved. Tree depth is 2/3 and potential discriminators (imaging, tumor markers) could further refine diagnosis."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.7, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.29438475519202795, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely explanation of this patient's current presentation?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Antacid overuse"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Increased 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Increased parathyroid hormone"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Malignancy"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Viral illness"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Hyperkalemia', 'Hyperparathyroidism'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.671}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "Hematologic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Lymphoma, Multiple Myeloma)", "posterior": 0.294, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B3", "B1.2", "B1.3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=75%, source=both]\n\nEndocrine/Paraneoplastic Hypercalcemia Syndrome vs Hematologic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Lymphoma, Multiple Myeloma):\n  Favours second: altered mental status, bone pain, confusion, constipation, decreased appetite\n\nEndocrine/Paraneoplastic Hypercalcemia Syndrome vs Metastatic Bone Disease with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Breast, Prostate Cancer):\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal circulating calcium concentration, infantile hypercalcemia\n\nHematologic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Lymphoma, Multiple Myeloma) vs Metastatic Bone Disease with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Breast, Prostate Cancer):\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal circulating calcium concentration, infantile hypercalcemia\n\nRelated to Endocrine/Paraneoplastic Hypercalcemia Syndrome: humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy, oncogenic osteomalacia, paraneoplastic neurologic syndrome\n\nRelated to Hematologic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Lymphoma, Multiple Myeloma): hypercalcemia disease, paraneoplastic syndrome\n\nRelated to Metastatic Bone Disease with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Breast, Prostate Cancer): calcium metabolic disease, familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia, humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy\n\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.55):]\n  Source: Update on multiple endocrine neoplasia Type 1 and 2. > History and Physical\n  It is also important to assess patients for hypercalcemia symptoms due to primary hyperparathyroidism and its associated clinical complications (eg, renal calculi and osteoporosis); signs and symptoms\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.57):]\n  Source: Hypercalcemia of Malignancy. > Differential Diagnosis\n  The following conditions should be considered when evaluating hypercalcemia:\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.75):]\n  Source: Hypercalcemia of Malignancy. > Pearls and Other Issues\n  Key facts to keep in mind regarding malignancy-related hypercalcemia include:\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.76):]\n  Source: Hypercalcemia of Malignancy. > Differential Diagnosis\n  The following conditions should be considered when evaluating hypercalcemia:\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  abdominal pain and trouble focusing → hypercalcemia → Hematologic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia (e.g., Lymphoma, Multiple Myeloma)\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate serum calcium levels to determine if hypercalcemia is present, as this would suggest a hematologic malignancy as the underlying cause\n  abdominal pain and poorly controlled GERD → gastric outlet obstruction → Solid Tumor with Paraneoplastic Hypercalcemia (e.g., Breast, Renal, Squamous Cell Carcinoma)\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Consider imaging to assess for gastric outlet obstruction as a potential mechanical complication of a GI tract malignancy\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
